Situation:
The division is conducting sustained offensive operations in a contested corridor. Forward units—Infantry Battalion Echo and Armored Cavalry Squadron Viper—are operating on separate axes with high daily consumption of the designated supply. Multiple rear-area depots (e.g., Supply Depot Bravo, Supply Depot Delta, Supply Depot Foxtrot) hold stocks but face uneven resupply and varying road access. The ground network includes valley corridors and river crossings exposed to ambush, indirect fire, and IED activity. Air resupply is constrained by weather and enemy air defense, making ground convoys the primary method. Route security requires engineer route clearance, EOD support, and armed escorts before any convoy can move.

Mission:
Move the designated supply from available depots to the forward units across the upcoming operational window to meet daily demand while minimizing total operational cost, to include route-specific transport costs, vehicle deployment costs, shortage penalties, and fixed route security risk costs.

Execution/Concept:
- Supply flow from each depot will not exceed available stocks for the period by depot and day. Movements will be scheduled daily to align with unit consumption and convoy protection windows.
- Unit demands are to be met daily; shortfalls are permitted only as a last resort and will incur explicit penalty costs. Shortfalls will be tracked and reported for immediate mitigation.
- All movements are ground-based. Each vehicle has a fixed carrying capacity; the volume of the supply item consumes a portion of that capacity per unit delivered. Vehicle allocations per route and day will be sized to meet demanded volume while respecting capacity limits and the total available fleet.
- Routes operate on an activate-to-use basis: activating a route (sending any vehicles between a specific depot and unit on a given day) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing escort teams, route clearance, and overwatch—this overhead applies regardless of the number of vehicles moved on that route that day. Only active routes may carry vehicles.
- Per-unit transport costs apply for each unit of supply moved along a given route; vehicle deployment costs apply per vehicle employed; unmet demand incurs penalty costs. All route, depot, and unit-specific data are provided in Annex A.
- The plan will concentrate flows onto a minimal set of secure routes where feasible to amortize fixed risk costs, while balancing transport costs, depot drawdowns, and unit demand timing. High-risk corridors will be used only when required to meet critical demand.
- Fleet employment will not exceed daily availability. Movement Control will allocate vehicles to active routes, coordinate with route clearance and security elements, and adjust activation decisions as conditions change.

